<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sabah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as North Borneo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sabah is a Malaysian state in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-kinabalu">Kota Kinabalu</a>. Long claimed by the sultanates of Brunei and Sulu, it was administered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-north-borneo-company">British North Borneo Company</a> from 1881, became a British crown colony in 1946 and joined Malaysia in 1963. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> still maintains a claim to its eastern part derived from Sulu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sabah.jpg" alt="Sabah" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mysid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sabah.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sabah lies in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, in the region of East Malaysia. It has land borders with Sarawak to the south-west and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>'s North Kalimantan province to the south, and the island of Labuan lies off its west coast. Its total land area is nearly 73,904 square kilometres, and it is surrounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>, the Sulu Sea and the Celebes Sea. The west is mountainous, with the Crocker Range, and Mount Kinabalu, about 4,095 metres high, is the highest point of both Sabah and Malaysia. The Kinabatangan River is the second longest in Malaysia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known human settlement lies along Darvel Bay, at the Madai-Baturong caves near the Tingkayu River, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> and food remains show habitation 20,000 to 30,000 years ago. The site at Skull Hill (Bukit Tengkorak) in Semporna District was the largest pottery-making site in Neolithic Southeast Asia. The state has traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> since the fourteenth century, and it came under the influence of Brunei in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> is said to have sent an expedition to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> in 1292, and many of his followers and other Chinese traders are believed to have settled at the Kinabatangan River.</p><p>In 1761 Alexander Dalrymple of the British East India Company agreed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Sulu to set up a trading post in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. By 1765 he had obtained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balambangan-island">Balambangan Island</a>, where a small British factory was founded in 1773, but the British abandoned it two years later when Sulu pirates attacked. The territory was subsequently acquired by the British North Borneo Chartered Company in the nineteenth century.</p><p>Japanese forces landed on Labuan on 3 January 1942 and occupied North Borneo until 1945. The Chinese resisted, forming the Kinabalu Guerillas under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-kwok">Albert Kwok</a>, and Kwok and many sympathisers were executed after the Jesselton Revolt. North Borneo became a British Crown colony on 15 July 1946. It attained self-governance on 31 August 1963, and on 16 September 1963, as Sabah, it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, Sarawak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> to form Malaysia, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cobbold-commission">Cobbold Commission</a> had found the union generally favoured.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-kinabalu">Kota Kinabalu</a> is the state capital. Sabah is a state of Malaysia, with a governor as head of state and a chief minister heading the government, in a system modelled on the Westminster parliament. Malay is the official language of the state and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> its state religion. The state has an estimated 42 ethnic groups, of which the largest indigenous ones are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadazan-dusun">Kadazan-Dusun</a>, the Bajau and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murut">Murut</a>. Its exports include oil, gas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sabah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its Malay court culture, titles and language shaped the coastal peoples of Sarawak and <b>Sabah</b>, and its brass cannon and gongs circulated widely in the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In May 1961 the Tunku proposed a wider federation including Singapore, <b>North Borneo</b>, Sarawak and Brunei, partly to balance Singapore's Chinese majority with the peoples of Borneo and to contain left-wing politics in Singapore.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1878 agreement under which Sultan Jamalul Alam granted <b>North Borneo</b> to Baron Gustav von Overbeck and Alfred Dent underlies the Philippine claim to Sabah, formally raised in 1962, and the claims of the sultan's heirs, which remain a source of friction between the Philippines and Malaysia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Talasea obsidian reached Vanuatu, New Caledonia and Fiji, thousands of kilometres to the south-east, and also travelled west: pieces have been identified at Bukit Tengkorak in <b>Sabah</b>, on the island of Borneo.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Some federations give all units the same powers; others are asymmetric, as Malaysia is, where <b>Sabah</b> and Sarawak joined on special terms.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>It was written in 1921, after the Jallianwala Bagh massacre and other atrocities by the British colonial rulers, and first published in the journal <b>Sabah</b>, published from Delhi.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sarfaroshi-ki-tamanna">Sarfaroshi Ki Tamanna</a></li></ul></section>
